# Day10
problem statement:
Some bash scripts need to be created to automate different day to day tasks. One is to create a bash script for taking websites backup. We have a static website running on App Server 1 , and we need to create a bash script named ecommerce_backup.sh which should accomplish the following tasks. (Also remember to place the script under /scripts directory on App Server 1).
  1. Create a zip archive named archive.zip of /var/www/html/ecommerce directory.
  2. Save the archive in /backup/ on App Server 1 (Consider it as application server). This is a temporary storage, as backups from this location will be cleaned on a weekly basis. Therefore, we also need to save this backup archive on Storage Server (Consider it as a backup server).
  3. Copy the created archive to Storage Server server in /backup/ location.
  4. Please make sure script won't ask for password while copying the archive file. Additionally, the respective server user (for example, tony in case of App Server 1) must be able to run it.
  5. Do not use sudo inside the script.
Note:
The zip package must be installed on given App Server before executing the script. This package is essential for creating the zip archive of the website files. Install it manually outside the script.

Solution:
  1. As per note section, zip package is not installed on application server, hence we will first install zip package.
      a.  Login into application server 1 using ssh, and provide password once prompted.
            ssh username@hostname
      b. It is assumed that application server is RHEL or centOS, we can install zip package using below package
            sudo yum install zip -y
  2. Once zip package is installed, next step is to establish password-less ssh connection between application server 1 and storage server. This can be done using steps provided in Day7.
  3. Once we have password-less ssh connection, next step is to write script. First we will create command one by one and then add those command into the script.
  4. To create zip of mentioned directory, we can use below zip command
      zip -r /backup/archive.zip /var/www/html/ecommerce       //Here -r flag denotes recursive achiving. /backup/archive.zip is the location and filename of the archive. /var/www/html/ecommerce is the directory of which archive will be made. Reference: https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/linux-unix/zip-command-in-linux-with-examples/ 
  5. Next and final step is to copy newly created archive to /backup/ directory on storage server. For this, we can use scp command
      scp /backup/archive.zip username@hostname:/backup/        // Here /backup/archive.zip is the source file to be copied. username is the username of user available on storage server. hostname is the computer name of storage server and /backup/ is the directory on storage server under which this archive needs to be copied. Reference: https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/scp.1.html
  6. Now, let's create ecommerce_backup.sh script under /scripts/ directory.
      cd /scripts
      vi ecommerce_backup.sh
  7. This will open vi editor in which we can provide below content
      #!/bin/bash                                                            //This is called as shebang. Reference: https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/linux-unix/using-shebang-in-linux/
      zip -r /backup/archive.zip /var/www/html/ecommerce        // Command from line #21
      scp /backup/archive.zip username@hostnme:/backup/          // Command from line #23
  8. Save file using "esc -> :wq -> enter
  9. Provide required permission to user to execute script
      chmod +755 archive.zip
  10. Try to execute script using below command
      ./ecommerce_backup.sh
